The three PAO-owned executables in each binary archive include Go runtime and
standard-library code. The candidate uses Go 1.27.3. Every binary archive
includes its exact toolchain's BSD-style license at licenses/GO-LICENSE.txt.

The host PAO executable also includes the Go 1.27.1 Zstandard decoder
from src/internal/zstd, Copyright The Go Authors, under the same
BSD-3-Clause terms. Its source, exact hashes and retained license are in
internal/zstd. Six upstream files are unchanged; PAO modifies zstd.go to
support windows up to 128 MiB and reject larger windows before allocation,
and bits.go to avoid overflow while reading wide reverse-bitstream values.
This decoder is used only for bounded database preparation.

Source-only development resources include unchanged CycloneDX schemas
(Apache-2.0), SPDX 2.3 schema (CC-BY-3.0), SARIF 2.1.0 schema
(OASIS notices), and eight MIT-licensed Python validator wheels. Their exact
sources, versions, hashes, attribution and license texts are retained in
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md, schemas/vendor/pins.json and schemas/vendor/licenses/. They are
not included in the binary payload. JSF/OpenKeyStore attribution remains in
the unchanged referenced schema.

The binary archive includes a PAO-authored Apache-2.0 sample Opengrep rule
pack with five rules, its license and its file manifest. External scanner
binaries, runtime images, third-party rule packs and advisory databases are
not bundled. Explicit online setup downloads separate inputs, including a
multi-license Python/Debian runtime and REUSE dependencies. Their licenses
and redistribution terms are tracked in docs/legal/toolchain-register.md
in the source distribution. No technical check substitutes for a human
security/license/publication decision.
